---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-in-south-africa`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D in South Africa',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Vitamin D deficiency', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'E55', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D in South Africa', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D in South Africa: Abundant Sun, Uneven Vitamin D Status"
  description="South Africa's low latitude means strong UVB most of the year, and deficiency is genuinely low in much of the population — but skin tone, urban life, and winter still create pockets of risk."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-in-south-africa", label: "Vitamin D in South Africa" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D in South Africa</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      South Africa sits at roughly 22–35°S, low enough that useful UVB reaches the ground for most of
      the year across most of the country. On the whole this is good news: population studies find that
      severe vitamin D deficiency is genuinely <em>less</em> common here than in Europe. But the picture
      is uneven, and it is a useful reminder that both extremes — plenty and shortfall — can coexist in
      one country.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Where the sun does its job</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      For much of South Africa's population, especially people who spend time outdoors, ambient UVB is
      sufficient to maintain adequate vitamin D through most of the year. This is the flip side of the
      high-latitude story told elsewhere on this site: closer to the tropics, the sun climbs high enough
      for skin synthesis across far more of the calendar, so a healthy outdoor lifestyle usually keeps
      levels up without supplements. See <a href="/vitamin-d-by-latitude" class="text-amber-700 underline">vitamin D by latitude</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where risk still concentrates</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Deficiency does appear in specific groups. Highly pigmented skin needs substantially more UVB for
      the same synthesis, so even under a strong sun some individuals with the darkest skin can run low
      if their exposure is limited. Urbanisation, indoor work, sun avoidance, and covering for cultural
      reasons all reduce exposure. The higher-altitude, cooler interior (the Highveld around
      Johannesburg) and the winter months in the far south (the Cape) create seasonal dips. Older adults
      and the housebound are, as everywhere, most vulnerable.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Diet, fortification, and testing</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      South Africa has mandatory fortification of maize meal and wheat flour with several micronutrients,
      though vitamin D is not the centrepiece of that program, so most vitamin D still comes from sun and
      a modest amount of diet. Routine screening of healthy people is not recommended; testing and
      treatment focus on at-risk and symptomatic individuals — the elderly, those with bone disease,
      malabsorption, or very limited sun exposure.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical takeaway</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Most active South Africans get adequate vitamin D from the abundant sun for much of the year.</li>
      <li>People with very dark skin and low sun exposure can still run low — the priority group.</li>
      <li>Winter in the Cape and indoor urban lifestyles create seasonal and situational risk.</li>
      <li>As always, protect against skin damage in the strong summer sun rather than deliberately over-exposing.</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> A sunny climate helps but does not guarantee sufficiency for
      everyone. If you have risk factors or symptoms, ask your doctor about a 25(OH)D test.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-in-south-africa" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
